#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Android for the Linux desktop (LNX-7): the desktop's side of what lujarch_linux lends it under
/run/lujarch (the daemon's bridge.h).

  android daemon          the resident part, started by the desktop's session
  android set KEY VALUE   flips one of Android's switches (what the quick settings' tiles run): wifi,
                          mobile, bluetooth, airplane, hotspot, flashlight, rotation, dnd, caffeine (1 or 0)
                          or brightness (the slider's position); exit 1 when Android's quick settings are
                          not lent
  android terminal        turns the phone from the desktop to the terminal (the navigation panel's Terminal
                          key, LNX-6); exit 1 when lujarch_linux does not read it

Android's switches and readings come from /run/lujarch/android, which the Terminal app keeps while Android's
quick settings are lent; the quick settings (org.lujarch.android in QML) read it themselves. The daemon puts
the phone behind the Linux interfaces programs already use:

- the session bus: org.kde.Solid.PowerManagement's BrightnessControl (the drawer's brightness slider, the
  name powerdevil owns when it runs, which the session turns off) and org.sigxcpu.Feedback (feedbackd's API:
  plasma-keyboard's key vibration, as Android's touch vibration, /run/lujarch/vibrate);
- the desktop's system bus (DBUS_SYSTEM_BUS_ADDRESS): org.freedesktop.UPower with the phone's battery, which
  the status bar and the battery tile read, and a GeoClue agent; while GeoClue has a client the daemon reads
  Android's fixes from /run/lujarch/location and hands them to GeoClue through its NMEA socket and its static
  source file.

Only the standard library: Linux has no D-Bus bindings here, and the part of D-Bus this needs is small.
"""
